window.TASKS = {"7":{"1":{"n":1,"title":"Browser-Controlled LED (HTTP Web Server)","sub":"ESP32 HTTP web server, WiFi client, GPIO2 control","meta":[["Board","ESP32"],["Protocol","HTTP over WiFi"],["Output","Built-in LED · GPIO2"],["Logic","Active HIGH · 3.3V"]],"parts":[{"terms":[["ESP32","The board this whole week is built on: a small, low-cost computer on a single chip with WiFi built in. It has two processor cores (dual-core) and 4MB of flash memory, and its pins let it read sensors and switch things on and off."],["WiFi client","Joining an existing WiFi network the way a phone does, instead of creating a network of its own. To join, the ESP32 needs the network’s name and its password."],["SSID","The name of a WiFi network: the name you pick from the WiFi list on your phone. The ESP32 needs it typed exactly, capital letters included."],["Signal","How strongly the ESP32 can hear the router. A weak signal makes the connection fail or drop, and the ESP32 can only join 2.4GHz networks."],["HTTP","The language a web browser and a web server use to talk. The browser sends a request, the server sends back an answer. Opening a page and pressing a button on it are both HTTP requests."],["Web server","A program that waits for requests and answers them. Here it runs on the ESP32 itself, so the board serves its own control page."],["Flash","The ESP32’s built-in permanent storage (4MB). The program, and the small control page it serves, are kept here and survive a power cut."],["Endpoint","One specific address on the server that does one job. In this task /on switches the LED on and /off switches it off. Typing the address into a browser does the same as pressing the button."],["GPIO","General-purpose input/output pin. A numbered pin on the board that the program can set HIGH (3.3V) or LOW (0V) to switch something, or read from."],["GPIO2","The pin that is already connected to the ESP32’s built-in LED, so this task needs no extra wires or parts."],["Built-in LED","The small light on the board itself. Its resistor is already fitted, so nothing else has to be added."],["Active HIGH","“on” means HIGH. Writing HIGH (3.3V) to GPIO2 turns the LED on and writing LOW turns it off. The opposite, active LOW, is what you meet with the relay in Task 2."],["Pull-based","How HTTP works: the server can never speak first. Every change needs a fresh request from the browser, so the page has to keep asking whether the LED is on. That is fine for occasional clicks, but too slow for anything real-time — the gap MQTT fills in Task 2."],["MQTT","A lightweight messaging protocol made for IoT, and the answer to HTTP being pull-based. Instead of asking again and again, a device subscribes to a topic once and is told the instant something is published to it. You build with it in Task 2."],["Serial Monitor","A window in the Arduino IDE that shows the text the board prints over the USB cable. Here it is how you read the address the ESP32 was given."]],"guide":[{"h":"Stage A — Get set up","steps":[{"t":"Gather what you need","d":["This task needs no extra components."],"list":["An ESP32 development board with a built-in LED on GPIO2 (most ESP32 DevKit-style boards have one — a small blue light near the USB socket).","A USB cable that carries data. Some cables only charge, and they will never show up as a port.","A computer (Windows, Mac or Linux).","A 2.4GHz WiFi network whose name and password you know.","A phone or laptop connected to that same WiFi."]},{"t":"Install the Arduino IDE","d":["Download Arduino IDE 2 from [arduino.cc/en/software](https://www.arduino.cc/en/software), run the installer and accept the defaults."]},{"t":"Teach the IDE about the ESP32","d":["Open File → Preferences (on a Mac: Arduino IDE → Settings). Find the box called “Additional boards manager URLs” and paste this address into it, then press OK:","`https://espressif.github.io/arduino-esp32/package_esp32_index.json`","Now open Tools → Board → Boards Manager (or click the board icon on the left), search for `esp32`, and install “esp32 by Espressif Systems”. It downloads a lot, so give it a few minutes."]},{"t":"Connect the board","d":["Plug the ESP32 into the computer. Choose Tools → Board → esp32 → ESP32 Dev Module. Then choose Tools → Port and pick the new port that appeared (something like COM3 on Windows)."],"tip":"No port appears? Try another cable or USB socket first. If it still does not show, install the USB driver for the small chip next to the USB socket — it is printed on it as CP210x or CH340."}]},{"h":"Stage B — Write and upload the program","steps":[{"t":"Paste the code","d":["Choose File → New Sketch, delete everything in it, and paste the code below. Then change the two lines at the top to your own WiFi name and password, keeping the quotation marks."],"code":{"name":"task1_led.ino","lang":"C++ · Arduino","text":"/* Task 1 - Browser-controlled LED\n   The ESP32 joins your WiFi, serves a small control page, and switches the\n   built-in LED (GPIO2) when the page asks it to. */\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <WebServer.h>\n\nconst char* WIFI_SSID     = \"YOUR_WIFI_NAME\";        // the SSID, exactly as your phone shows it\nconst char* WIFI_PASSWORD = \"YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD\";\n\nconst int LED_PIN = 2;      // GPIO2 is wired to the built-in LED\nWebServer server(80);       // port 80 is the normal web port\nbool ledOn = false;\n\n// The control page. It is stored in the ESP32's flash and sent to the browser as-is.\nconst char PAGE[] PROGMEM = R\"HTML(<!doctype html>\n<html><head>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n<title>ESP32 LED</title>\n<style>\n  body{font-family:sans-serif;text-align:center;margin-top:60px}\n  button{font-size:22px;padding:14px 34px;margin:8px;border:0;border-radius:8px}\n  #s{font-size:20px;margin-top:22px}\n</style></head><body>\n<h2>ESP32 built-in LED</h2>\n<button onclick=\"send('on')\">ON</button>\n<button onclick=\"send('off')\">OFF</button>\n<div id=\"s\">LED is ...</div>\n<script>\n  function show(t){ document.getElementById('s').textContent = 'LED is ' + t; }\n  function send(path){ fetch('/' + path).then(r => r.text()).then(show); }\n  fetch('/state').then(r => r.text()).then(show);\n</script>\n</body></html>)HTML\";\n\nvoid sendState() { server.send(200, \"text/plain\", ledOn ? \"ON\" : \"OFF\"); }\nvoid handleRoot() { server.send(200, \"text/html\", PAGE); }\nvoid handleOn()  { ledOn = true;  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH); sendState(); }   // active HIGH: HIGH = on\nvoid handleOff() { ledOn = false; digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);  sendState(); }\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);\n\n  Serial.print(\"Connecting to WiFi\");\n  WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA);                       // act as a WiFi client\n  WiFi.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASSWORD);\n  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {\n    delay(500);\n    Serial.print(\".\");\n  }\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.println(\"Connected. Open this address in a browser:\");\n  Serial.print(\"http://\");\n  Serial.println(WiFi.localIP());\n\n  server.on(\"/\", handleRoot);                // the page\n  server.on(\"/on\", handleOn);                // endpoint: LED on\n  server.on(\"/off\", handleOff);              // endpoint: LED off\n  server.on(\"/state\", sendState);            // endpoint: is the LED on?\n  server.begin();\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  server.handleClient();                     // answer any waiting browser request\n}\n"}},{"t":"Upload it","d":["Click the right-arrow Upload button. Watch the black area at the bottom: it prints “Connecting......” and then starts writing. Wait for “Done uploading”."],"tip":"Stuck on “Connecting......”? Press and hold the BOOT button on the board until the text changes to “Writing at…”, then let go."},{"t":"Read the address from the Serial Monitor","d":["Open Tools → Serial Monitor. In its bottom-right corner set the speed to 115200 baud, then press the EN (or RESET) button on the board. After a few seconds you should see the board join the WiFi and print its address."],"expect":"Connecting to WiFi....\nConnected. Open this address in a browser:\nhttp://192.168.1.42\n(Your numbers will be different. That is normal.)"}]},{"h":"Stage C — Control the LED from a browser","steps":[{"t":"Open the page","d":["On your phone or laptop — connected to the same WiFi as the ESP32 — open a browser and type the address exactly as the Serial Monitor printed it. It must start with `http://`, not `https://`."],"expect":"A plain page titled “ESP32 built-in LED” with an ON button, an OFF button and a line saying whether the LED is on."},{"t":"Press the buttons","d":["Press ON: the built-in LED lights up. Press OFF: it goes dark. Try it from across the room — that is hardware answering to software over a network."]},{"t":"See what the buttons really do","d":["Each button simply opens an endpoint. Type the address followed by `/on` into the browser (for example `http://192.168.1.42/on`) and the LED switches on, exactly as if you had pressed ON. Use `/off` to switch it off. Every state change is a brand-new request — that is what “pull-based” means."]},{"t":"If something does not work","d":[],"table":{"head":["What you see","What it usually is","What to do"],"rows":[["Only dots, never “Connected”","Wrong WiFi name or password, a 5GHz-only network, or a weak signal","Check spelling and capital letters, use a 2.4GHz network, move closer to the router. A phone hotspot is a good quick test."],["“Failed to connect” while uploading","The board is not in upload mode, or the wrong port or cable","Hold the BOOT button while it connects, check Tools → Port, try another data cable."],["The page will not open","Phone on mobile data or another network, or `https://` typed","Join the same WiFi as the ESP32 and use `http://`."],["The page opens but the LED does nothing","Your board’s LED is on a different pin","Look up your board’s pinout and change `LED_PIN` at the top of the code."],["The address is different next time","The router gave the board a new address","Normal. Press EN and read the Serial Monitor again."]]}}]}],"story":["The week started with a simple question I asked myself: can I turn this ESP32 into a web server? I joined it to the WiFi with the SSID and password, served a small page straight from the board’s flash, and exposed a few endpoints that toggled the built-in LED on GPIO2. Then I pulled out my phone — and the LED switched on from across the room. It was a tiny thing, but it was my first time making hardware answer to software over a network.","The ESP32 is a proper little computer (dual-core, 4MB of flash, WiFi built in), and the LED needed no extra components at all since the resistor and LED are already on the board — it’s active HIGH, so writing 3.3V turns it on. The insight that shaped the rest of the week came from watching how the page behaved: every state change needed a fresh request, because HTTP is pull-based.","The browser had to keep asking whether the LED was on. That works fine for occasional clicks, but it made me realise that anything truly real-time would need a different, push-based protocol — the exact gap Task 2 filled with MQTT."],"media":[{"type":"image","title":"Task 1 — Built-in LED","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-1/1.jpg","caption":"The ESP32 serving its first control page — a button in the browser toggles the built-in LED on GPIO2.","poster":null,"w":1600,"h":900,"v":[640,1280]},{"type":"video","title":"Task 1 — LED demo","src":"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/JayasimhanSiva/PORTFOLIO@main/assets/protosem/week-7/task-1/2.mp4","caption":"Browser → WiFi → ESP32 → LED, live from across the room.","duration":"19","poster":"assets/protosem/week-7/posters/task-1-led-demo-poster.jpg","pw":1280,"ph":720}],"notes":["The very first blink from my browser honestly felt like a small victory. I connected the ESP32 to WiFi with the SSID and password, served a tiny page from its flash, and pressed a button on my phone screen to turn the built-in LED on and off. What I noticed watching it work was how the page kept polling the server to know the current state — and that was when I realized HTTP is pull-based, fine for occasional clicks but wrong for anything that must react instantly. That one observation is what drove me to MQTT in Task 2, and honestly, thinking about it, it is the whole reason the project got real."]}]},"2":{"n":2,"title":"Cloud MQTT Dashboard & 230V Relay Control","sub":"Adafruit IO, 230V relay switching, high-voltage safety","meta":[["Protocol","MQTT"],["Broker","Adafruit IO"],["Load","230V AC · 60W bulb"],["Switch","5V relay module"]],"parts":[{"terms":[["IoT","Internet of Things: everyday devices connected to the internet so they can be read and controlled from anywhere. This is the moment the project stopped being local and became real IoT."],["Cloud","Computers on the internet that sit between your dashboard and your ESP32. Here that is Adafruit IO."],["MQTT","A lightweight messaging protocol made for IoT. Devices do not talk to each other directly: they send messages through a broker. It is the protocol behind Philips Hue, Ecobee, Tesla and industrial platforms like AWS IoT Core."],["Publish–subscribe","One side publishes a message to a named topic, and everyone subscribed to that topic receives it instantly. The ESP32 subscribes once and hears every change the moment it happens, with no polling."],["Polling","Asking “any updates?” over and over. It is what the page in Task 1 has to do, and exactly what MQTT avoids."],["Topic","The name a message is published under, like a channel. On Adafruit IO it looks like `username/feeds/bulb`."],["Broker","The server that receives every published message and passes it on to whoever is subscribed. Here it is the MQTT broker at Adafruit IO."],["QoS","Quality of Service: how hard MQTT tries to deliver a message. Level 0 sends it at most once, level 1 at least once, level 2 exactly once."],["Adafruit IO","A free cloud service that gives you an MQTT broker, feeds and dashboards in one place."],["Feed","A named data channel in Adafruit IO that holds the latest value. This task uses one feed called `bulb` that holds ON or OFF."],["Dashboard","A web page made of blocks — buttons, gauges, charts — each linked to a feed. A toggle block writes ON or OFF into its feed when you flip it."],["Relay","An electrically operated switch. A small signal from the ESP32 makes a coil click a switch that can carry a much bigger load, and the two sides stay electrically separate."],["5V relay module","A ready-made board carrying the relay, its driver, an indicator LED and screw terminals. It runs from 5V. Three pins go to the ESP32 (VCC, GND, IN) and three screw terminals do the switching (COM, NO, NC)."],["230V AC (mains)","The voltage in a wall socket. It can kill. This is the first time in the program that you work with it."],["Live and Neutral","The two wires that bring mains power to a bulb. Live carries the dangerous voltage and Neutral is the return path. You switch only the Live wire, and Neutral goes straight to the bulb."],["Logic level","The voltage on a signal wire that means 0 or 1. On the ESP32 a HIGH is 3.3V."],["Bench test","Testing everything at a safe low voltage (5V) on the desk before any 230V wiring exists."],["Active LOW / polarity","Which signal level switches a relay on. Many relay modules switch ON when their input is pulled LOW — the opposite of the LED in Task 1. The relay in this task did exactly that, which is why you always check polarity."],["Datasheet","The maker’s document that says exactly how a part behaves. Read it before you trust polarity, pins or voltages."],["Safe OFF state","The firmware always starts with the relay OFF, so a reset, a power cut or a lost connection can never leave the bulb switched on by surprise."]],"guide":[{"h":"Stage A — Safety first","steps":[{"t":"Read this before you touch anything","d":["This task ends with a real 230V, 60W bulb. Stages B and C use only 5V and are completely safe. Stage D is the only stage that touches mains, and it has to follow these rules."],"warn":["230V AC can kill. Do the mains part only with a trained person or teacher present — never alone.","Change any wiring only when the plug is out of the wall socket.","Finish and prove every low-voltage step (Stage C) before any mains wire exists.","Use properly insulated wire and screw terminals, keep no bare copper exposed, and close the relay module and its joints inside an insulated box.","Switch only the Live wire. Neutral runs straight from the plug to the bulb.","Keep your hands, the floor and the table dry, and never touch the relay’s mains-side terminals while the plug is in."]}]},{"h":"Stage B — Set up the cloud side (Adafruit IO)","steps":[{"t":"Create an account","d":["Go to [io.adafruit.com](https://io.adafruit.com), choose Sign Up, and make a free account. Confirm the email they send you and sign in."]},{"t":"Create the feed","d":["Open Feeds → New Feed. Name it `bulb` and press Create. The name matters: the code below uses exactly `bulb`."]},{"t":"Create the dashboard and the toggle","d":["Open Dashboards → New Dashboard, name it `Smart Home`, and open it. Click the gear icon in the top right and choose Create New Block. Pick Toggle, tick the feed `bulb`, press Next, leave the button texts as `ON` and `OFF`, and press Create Block."]},{"t":"Copy your username and key","d":["Click the yellow key icon (AIO Key) at the top of the page. A box shows your Username and your Active Key. Copy both somewhere safe."],"warn":["The key works like a password. Never post it in a public place."]}]},{"h":"Stage C — Build and test at 5V (no mains yet)","steps":[{"t":"Gather the parts","d":[],"list":["The ESP32 and its USB cable.","A 5V relay module (one channel).","Three jumper wires.","For Stage D only: a 230V 60W bulb in a holder with a cord and plug, and an insulated box."]},{"t":"Wire the relay module to the ESP32","d":["Leave the three screw terminals (COM, NO, NC) empty for now. Connect only the three pins:"],"table":{"head":["Relay module","ESP32"],"rows":[["VCC","VIN (the 5V that comes from the USB cable)"],["GND","GND"],["IN","GPIO26"]]}},{"t":"Install the MQTT library","d":["In the Arduino IDE open Sketch → Include Library → Manage Libraries, search for `PubSubClient`, and install the one by Nick O’Leary."]},{"t":"Paste the code and fill in four values","d":["Create a new sketch and paste this code. Fill in your WiFi name, your WiFi password, your Adafruit IO username and your Adafruit IO key. Upload it exactly as in Task 1, then open the Serial Monitor at 115200 baud."],"expect":"WiFi connected\nConnecting to MQTT... connected\nSubscribed to your_username/feeds/bulb","code":{"name":"task2_relay.ino","lang":"C++ · Arduino","text":"/* Task 2 - Cloud MQTT relay\n   The ESP32 subscribes to an Adafruit IO feed called \"bulb\". When the dashboard\n   toggle publishes ON or OFF, the relay switches. It always starts OFF. */\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <PubSubClient.h>\n\nconst char* WIFI_SSID     = \"YOUR_WIFI_NAME\";\nconst char* WIFI_PASSWORD = \"YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD\";\nconst char* AIO_USERNAME  = \"YOUR_ADAFRUIT_IO_USERNAME\";\nconst char* AIO_KEY       = \"YOUR_ADAFRUIT_IO_KEY\";\n\nconst char* MQTT_SERVER = \"io.adafruit.com\";\nconst int   MQTT_PORT   = 1883;\n\nconst int RELAY_PIN = 26;\nconst int RELAY_ON  = LOW;     // most 5V relay modules are active LOW ...\nconst int RELAY_OFF = HIGH;    // ... swap these two lines if yours is the opposite\n\nString topic;                  // becomes YOUR_USERNAME/feeds/bulb\nWiFiClient wifiClient;\nPubSubClient mqtt(wifiClient);\n\nvoid setRelay(bool on) {\n  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, on ? RELAY_ON : RELAY_OFF);\n  Serial.println(on ? \"Relay ON\" : \"Relay OFF\");\n}\n\n// Runs every time a message arrives on the topic.\nvoid onMessage(char* messageTopic, byte* payload, unsigned int length) {\n  String msg;\n  for (unsigned int i = 0; i < length; i++) msg += (char)payload[i];\n  msg.trim();\n  msg.toUpperCase();\n  Serial.print(\"Received: \");\n  Serial.println(msg);\n  if (msg == \"ON\" || msg == \"1\") setRelay(true);\n  else if (msg == \"OFF\" || msg == \"0\") setRelay(false);\n}\n\nvoid connectWiFi() {\n  Serial.print(\"Connecting to WiFi\");\n  WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA);\n  WiFi.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASSWORD);\n  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {\n    delay(500);\n    Serial.print(\".\");\n  }\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.println(\"WiFi connected\");\n}\n\nvoid connectMqtt() {\n  while (!mqtt.connected()) {\n    Serial.print(\"Connecting to MQTT... \");\n    String clientId = \"esp32-\" + String((uint32_t)ESP.getEfuseMac(), HEX);\n    if (mqtt.connect(clientId.c_str(), AIO_USERNAME, AIO_KEY)) {\n      Serial.println(\"connected\");\n      mqtt.subscribe(topic.c_str());\n      Serial.print(\"Subscribed to \");\n      Serial.println(topic);\n    } else {\n      Serial.print(\"failed, code \");\n      Serial.print(mqtt.state());\n      Serial.println(\" - trying again in 5 s\");\n      delay(5000);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);     // choose the OFF level first ...\n  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);             // ... then make the pin an output, so the relay never blips on at boot\n\n  topic = String(AIO_USERNAME) + \"/feeds/bulb\";\n  connectWiFi();\n  mqtt.setServer(MQTT_SERVER, MQTT_PORT);\n  mqtt.setCallback(onMessage);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  if (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {\n    setRelay(false);                      // lost the network: fall back to the safe OFF state\n    connectWiFi();\n  }\n  if (!mqtt.connected()) {\n    setRelay(false);                      // lost the broker: safe OFF as well\n    connectMqtt();\n  }\n  mqtt.loop();                            // keeps the connection alive and delivers messages\n}\n"}},{"t":"Test with the dashboard","d":["Flip the toggle on your Adafruit IO dashboard. The Serial Monitor prints `Received: ON`, the relay clicks and the small light on the relay module comes on. Flip it back: it prints `Received: OFF` and the relay clicks off."]},{"t":"Check the polarity","d":["Many relay modules switch ON when their input goes LOW. If yours clicks on when the dashboard says OFF, swap the two lines `RELAY_ON` and `RELAY_OFF` in the code and upload again. Read your module’s datasheet or the labels on the board before you trust either level."],"tip":"This is not a small detail. Guessing the polarity is how a relay ends up switched on when you think it is off."},{"t":"Prove the safe OFF state","d":["Press the EN button on the ESP32. The relay must stay off while the board restarts and stay off until you flip the toggle. Only when every test above passes do you move on to mains."]}]},{"h":"Stage D — Wire the 230V bulb","steps":[{"t":"Unplug everything first","d":["Unplug the ESP32 and make sure no mains plug is in a socket. The relay’s three screw terminals are COM (common), NO (normally open) and NC (normally closed). Use COM and NO: NO is open when the relay is off, so the bulb is off whenever the relay is not switched on."],"warn":["Nothing in this stage is done with power connected."]},{"t":"Wire only the Live conductor through the relay","d":[],"tip":"If the plug has an Earth wire, it goes straight through and is never switched.","table":{"head":["From","To"],"rows":[["Live wire of the plug","COM on the relay"],["NO on the relay","Live terminal of the bulb holder"],["Neutral wire of the plug","Neutral terminal of the bulb holder, directly (it never goes through the relay)"]]}},{"t":"Check every joint, then close the box","d":["Tighten each screw, gently tug each wire to prove it is held, and confirm no bare copper is visible. Put the relay module and the mains joints inside the insulated box and close it."]},{"t":"Power up in the right order","d":["Power the ESP32 from USB first — the bulb must be off. Then plug the mains cord in. Now flip the dashboard toggle: the relay clicks and the bulb lights. Flip it back: the bulb goes out."],"expect":"The bulb follows the dashboard toggle from anywhere with internet — no shared WiFi needed."},{"t":"Whenever you want to change something","d":["Pull the mains plug out of the wall first. Every time."]}]}],"story":["Task 2 was where the project stopped being local and became real IoT. I moved control from my home network to the cloud over MQTT through Adafruit IO, so a toggle in a dashboard could now switch a bulb from anywhere on Earth. MQTT is publish-subscribe — the ESP32 subscribes to a topic and the moment anyone publishes, it hears it instantly with no polling. This is the same protocol that runs Philips Hue, Ecobee, Tesla, and industrial platforms like AWS IoT Core.","The physical side was the big deal for me: I connected a 5V relay module to switch a real 230V, 60W bulb. This was my first time touching mains voltage, so I treated it with real discipline — I verified every logic level at safe 5V on the bench before any 230V wiring existed, switched only the Live wire and left Neutral direct to the bulb, and the firmware started in the safe OFF state every time.","Thanks to that process, I finished with zero safety incidents. The relay caught me once though: switching a low GPIO made it turn ON, the opposite of what felt intuitive, so from then on I always read the datasheet before trusting polarity."],"media":[{"type":"image","title":"Task 2 — Relay & bulb wiring","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-2/1.jpg","caption":"The 5V relay module switching a real 230V, 60W bulb — every logic level verified on the bench before touching mains.","poster":null,"w":1600,"h":1200,"v":[640,1280]},{"type":"image","title":"Task 2 — Adafruit IO dashboard","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-2/2.jpg","caption":"Cloud MQTT control: the dashboard toggle publishes to Adafruit IO and the bulb follows — from anywhere.","poster":null,"w":1600,"h":1200,"v":[640,1280]},{"type":"video","title":"Task 2 — Cloud bulb demo","src":"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/JayasimhanSiva/PORTFOLIO@main/assets/protosem/week-7/task-2/3.mp4","caption":"The dashboard flip reaching the bulb over MQTT, with no local network needed.","duration":"15","poster":"assets/protosem/week-7/posters/task-2-mqtt-demo-poster.jpg","pw":1280,"ph":720}],"notes":["MQTT clicked for me on Task 2. Instead of asking the server “any updates?” every few hundred milliseconds, the ESP32 just subscribed to a topic and heard the change the instant it happened. Moving control to the cloud through Adafruit IO meant I could switch a bulb from anywhere, and switching that bulb meant working with 230V for the first time. I made a ritual of safety: verify every logic level at 5V on the bench first, switch only the Live conductor, start safe-OFF. The relay surprised me once — pulling the pin LOW switched it ON, not HIGH — and that near-miss is exactly why I now read datasheets before trusting anything with mains. Zero incidents by the end."]}]},"3":{"n":3,"title":"Google Assistant Voice Control via IFTTT","sub":"IFTTT applets, webhooks, multi-platform chain","meta":[["Voice","Google Assistant"],["Automation","IFTTT applets"],["Chain","Webhook → Adafruit IO → MQTT"],["Rate limit","~1 command / 30 s"]],"parts":[{"terms":[["Google Assistant","Google’s voice assistant. It listens for a phrase and hands it to the next platform in the chain."],["IFTTT","“If This Then That”: a service that connects other services. When something happens in one, it makes something happen in another."],["Applet","One IFTTT automation. Here each applet says: if I say this exact phrase to Google Assistant, then send this web request."],["Phrase","The exact words you say. They have to match the applet’s phrase perfectly."],["Webhook","A web address that another service calls to make something happen. IFTTT calls one to write into your Adafruit IO feed."],["POST","A kind of web request that sends data to a server. The webhook uses it to put ON or OFF into the feed."],["Adafruit IO feed","The `bulb` feed from Task 2. The webhook writes to it, and the ESP32 is subscribed to it."],["MQTT","The messaging protocol from Task 2. It carries the new feed value from Adafruit IO to the ESP32 within a moment."],["Relay","The switch from Task 2 that clicks the real bulb on and off."],["Chain","The five platforms working in a row: Google Assistant, IFTTT, the webhook, Adafruit IO and MQTT. If any one link fails, the whole chain silently fails."],["Rate limit","How often a service allows a command. IFTTT lets an applet fire about once every 30 seconds, so a second command sent too quickly is skipped."],["Completed","The status IFTTT shows in an applet’s activity when it ran successfully."],["Stale feed value","The feed still holds an old value, so what you see there is not what you expect. It hides whether your last command arrived."],["Dead listener","The ESP32 has stopped listening because its MQTT connection dropped. The feed still changes, but nothing reacts."],["Serial Monitor","The Arduino IDE window that shows what the ESP32 prints. In this task it is how you see the message arrive."],["Troubleshooting map","A small table that tracks each symptom back to its cause and its fix. It is at the end of the steps."]],"guide":[{"h":"Stage A — Before you start","steps":[{"t":"Check what has to work already","d":["Task 3 adds no new hardware and no new code. It reuses the Task 2 build exactly as it is, so first make sure the Adafruit IO toggle really switches the bulb. If it does not, go back to Task 2."],"list":["The Task 2 build powered and connected, with the Serial Monitor open.","A phone with Google Assistant, signed in to your Google account.","Your Adafruit IO username and key.","An IFTTT account (free to start)."]},{"t":"Keep your key private","d":["The web address you build below contains your Adafruit IO key. Treat that address like a password and do not share it or post it in public."]}]},{"h":"Stage B — Create the “bulb on” applet","steps":[{"t":"Start a new applet","d":["Go to [ifttt.com](https://ifttt.com), sign in, and choose Create. Under “If This” press Add."]},{"t":"Choose what you say","d":["Search for `Google Assistant`, connect it to your Google account and allow access. Pick “Say a simple phrase” and fill it in:"],"tip":"IFTTT’s screens change slightly over time. If a label looks different, pick the one that means the same thing.","table":{"head":["Field","Value"],"rows":[["What do you want to say?","`activate bulb on`"],["What do you want the Assistant to say in response?","`Turning the bulb on`"],["Language","English"]]}},{"t":"Choose what happens","d":["Under “Then That” press Add, search for `Webhooks`, and pick “Make a web request”. Fill it in, replacing `YOUR_USERNAME` and `YOUR_KEY` with the two values you copied from Adafruit IO:"],"table":{"head":["Field","Value"],"rows":[["URL","`https://io.adafruit.com/api/v2/YOUR_USERNAME/feeds/bulb/data?X-AIO-Key=YOUR_KEY`"],["Method","`POST`"],["Content Type","`application/json`"],["Body","`{\"value\":\"ON\"}`"]]}},{"t":"Save it","d":["Press Create action, then Continue, give it a title such as `Bulb ON`, and press Finish. Make sure the applet shows as connected."]}]},{"h":"Stage C — Create the “bulb off” applet","steps":[{"t":"Repeat with the opposite values","d":["Make a second applet exactly the same way, but change three things:"],"table":{"head":["Field","Value"],"rows":[["Phrase","`activate bulb off`"],["Assistant response","`Turning the bulb off`"],["Body","`{\"value\":\"OFF\"}`"]]}}]},{"h":"Stage D — Test the whole chain","steps":[{"t":"Run the seven-step test","d":["Say “Hey Google, activate bulb on” to your phone, then check every link in order. When all seven line up, the chain works."],"list":["Speak the phrase.","Confirm in the Google app that Assistant understood your words and answered.","Open IFTTT, open the applet’s Activity, and check it says Completed.","Open the `bulb` feed in Adafruit IO and check the newest value is ON.","Read the Serial Monitor: it prints `Received: ON`.","Listen: the relay clicks.","Look: the bulb is on."]},{"t":"Turn it off the same way","d":["Wait about 30 seconds — IFTTT’s rate limit — then say “Hey Google, activate bulb off” and walk through the same seven checks. The bulb should go out."],"expect":"A real bulb switches on and off across the room when you speak."},{"t":"If it does not work: the troubleshooting map","d":[],"table":{"head":["What you see","What it usually is","What to do"],"rows":[["Assistant does something else, or says it cannot help","The phrase does not match","Say the applet’s phrase exactly, and check its spelling in IFTTT."],["Assistant answers, but IFTTT shows no new activity","The applet was skipped (rate limit) or is switched off","Wait about 30 seconds between commands, and check the applet is on and your Google account is connected."],["IFTTT says Completed, but the feed did not change","A wrong username, key or feed name in the webhook","Re-check the URL: your username, your key, and the feed name `bulb`."],["The feed changed, but the relay did not move","Dead listener: the ESP32 is not connected to MQTT","Look at the Serial Monitor, press EN on the board, and check its WiFi."],["Nothing seems to happen, and the feed already says ON","Stale feed value","Say the opposite command first, then try again, and watch the newest value in the feed."]]}}]}],"story":["Honestly, voice control still felt like magic when it worked. I said “Hey Google, activate bulb on” and a real light across the room switched on. But the magic was really five independent platforms agreeing to behave: Google Assistant understood my words, IFTTT ran an applet for that exact phrase, a webhook POSTed my Adafruit IO feed, MQTT delivered it to the ESP32, and the relay clicked the bulb on — the whole chain in a couple of seconds.","Getting those five systems to trust each other was the hardest part of the week, and nothing about it was about writing clever code. Phrases had to match exactly, the applet had to fire within IFTTT’s rate limit of about one command every 30 seconds, and a single stale feed value or dead listener silently broke everything. I built a small troubleshooting map to track each symptom to its fix, and I followed a strict test ritual — speak, confirm in the Google app, watch the applet mark Completed, check the feed changed, read the serial monitor, hear the relay, see the bulb. When all seven steps lined up, I understood why real devices mix protocols the way they do."],"media":[{"type":"video","title":"Task 3 — Voice control demo","src":"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/JayasimhanSiva/PORTFOLIO@main/assets/protosem/week-7/task-3/1.mp4","caption":"“Hey Google, activate bulb on” — five platforms, one chain, a real bulb switching on across the room.","duration":"8","poster":"assets/protosem/week-7/posters/task-3-voice-demo-poster.jpg","pw":406,"ph":720}],"notes":["Voice control was the task I most wanted to show people. Standing there and saying “Hey Google, activate bulb on” while a real bulb turned on two metres away felt like the future — but I knew by then it was really five platforms doing exactly what I configured. The phrase had to match perfectly, IFTTT allowed about one command every 30 seconds, and each of Adafruit, webhooks, and MQTT had to stay healthy or the whole chain silently failed. I followed a seven-step ritual to trust the system: watch Google confirm, see the applet Complete, check the feed, read the serial line, hear the relay, see the bulb. I also wrote a troubleshooting map for the failure modes — wrong phrase, skipped applet, stale feed, dead listener — and it genuinely stopped me losing a day to a mystery."]}]},"4":{"n":4,"title":"Forge: Sensors, Cloud & Dashboard","sub":"DHT11 + LDR sensors, Firebase Realtime Database, responsive web dashboard","meta":[["Sensors","DHT11 + LDR"],["Cloud","Firebase Realtime Database"],["Interface","3-page web dashboard"],["Output","Relay · CSV export"]],"parts":[{"terms":[["Forge","The name of the system built across Tasks 4 and 5: an ESP32 that monitors the room 24/7, syncs to the cloud, and switches a real bulb from a live dashboard."],["Environmental station","The ESP32 reading temperature and humidity from a DHT11 and ambient light from an LDR, with the same relay from Task 2 controlling the bulb."],["DHT11","A digital temperature and humidity sensor. It sends its numbers as digital data and needs at least two seconds between reads. It also drifts quietly unless you average."],["LDR","Light-dependent resistor. Its resistance changes with the light falling on it, so it turns brightness into a voltage the ESP32 can read."],["Digital and analog sensor","A digital sensor (DHT11) hands over ready-made numbers. An analog sensor (LDR) gives a voltage that changes smoothly, and the ESP32 has to measure it."],["ADC","Analog-to-digital converter: the part of the ESP32 that turns a voltage into a number. It is 12-bit, so it reads 0–4095. An Arduino’s is 10-bit (0–1023), so thresholds copied from an Arduino would be off scale."],["Noise","Readings that jump for no reason. The raw light value went 500, then 2000, then 300."],["15-sample averaging","Instead of trusting one reading, take 15 readings 8ms apart and use their average. It gave a stable value, and better accuracy than the hardware filter capacitor first considered."],["Capacitor (hardware filter)","A small component that smooths a signal. It was the first idea for the noise, but it is inflexible; averaging in software is easier to change."],["Manual override","The bulb follows the toggle on the dashboard, whatever the light is doing."],["Automatic mode","The ESP32 decides on its own by watching the light and switching the bulb when the light crosses a threshold."],["Threshold","The light level, anywhere from 0 to 4095, at which automatic mode switches the bulb. It can be tuned from the dashboard."],["Data point","One reading of temperature, humidity, light and relay state. One every two seconds is 43,200 data points a day."],["Sync","Getting the data to the cloud. Testing showed steady readings, about a 1–2 second sync, and a relay response of around 10ms."],["Default OFF","If anything disconnects, the relay goes back to OFF."]],"guide":[{"h":"Stage A — Gather and wire","steps":[{"t":"Gather the parts","d":[],"list":["The ESP32, USB cable and Arduino IDE from Task 1.","A DHT11 temperature and humidity sensor (a bare sensor or a 3-pin module).","An LDR (light-dependent resistor) and one 10kΩ resistor.","The 5V relay module from Task 2.","A breadboard and jumper wires.","For the final bulb test: the mains bulb setup from Task 2, with the same safety rules."]},{"t":"Wire the DHT11","d":[],"tip":"A bare 4-pin sensor also needs a 10kΩ resistor between DATA and 3V3. A 3-pin module already has it built in.","table":{"head":["DHT11","ESP32"],"rows":[["VCC (+)","3V3"],["DATA (out)","GPIO4"],["GND (−)","GND"]]}},{"t":"Wire the LDR","d":["Build a small voltage divider on the breadboard. Connect one end of the 10kΩ resistor to 3V3. Connect its other end to one leg of the LDR and to GPIO34. Connect the other leg of the LDR to GND."],"tip":"With this wiring, the darker it gets the higher the number. GPIO34 is used on purpose: it is an ADC1 pin, and ADC2 pins cannot be read while WiFi is on.","table":{"head":["From","To"],"rows":[["3V3","10kΩ resistor, first end"],["10kΩ resistor, second end","LDR leg 1 and GPIO34"],["LDR leg 2","GND"]]}},{"t":"Wire the relay module","d":[],"table":{"head":["Relay module","ESP32"],"rows":[["VCC","VIN"],["GND","GND"],["IN","GPIO26"]]},"warn":["Leave the relay’s mains terminals empty for now. Bench-test everything at 5V first, exactly as in Task 2."]}]},{"h":"Stage B — Read the sensors and prove the averaging","steps":[{"t":"Install the DHT library","d":["In Sketch → Include Library → Manage Libraries, search for `DHT sensor library` by Adafruit and install it. When it offers to install the libraries it depends on, choose Install all (that adds Adafruit Unified Sensor)."]},{"t":"Paste and upload the hardware test","d":["Paste this code into a new sketch and upload it. It uses no WiFi and no cloud yet: it only proves the hardware, and prints everything to the Serial Monitor."],"expect":"Temp 27.4 C | Humidity 58 % | Light raw 2210 -> averaged 1934 | Relay OFF","code":{"name":"task4_sensors.ino","lang":"C++ · Arduino","text":"/* Task 4.1 - Forge hardware test\n   Reads the DHT11 (temperature + humidity) and the LDR (light), averages the\n   light over 15 samples, and switches the relay on its own when the light\n   reading is above a threshold. Everything is printed to the Serial Monitor.\n   No WiFi and no cloud yet - this proves the hardware first. */\n\n#include <DHT.h>\n\nconst int DHT_PIN   = 4;       // DHT11 data\nconst int LDR_PIN   = 34;      // LDR divider (an ADC1 pin, so it also works once WiFi is on)\nconst int RELAY_PIN = 26;      // relay module IN\nconst int RELAY_ON  = LOW;     // swap if your relay module is the opposite (see Task 2)\nconst int RELAY_OFF = HIGH;\n\nconst int SAMPLES       = 15;  // readings averaged into one light value\nconst int SAMPLE_GAP_MS = 8;   // pause between two samples\nint threshold = 2000;          // 0-4095: relay ON above this light reading (tune it later)\n\nDHT dht(DHT_PIN, DHT11);\n\nint readLightAveraged() {\n  long sum = 0;\n  for (int i = 0; i < SAMPLES; i++) {\n    sum += analogRead(LDR_PIN);\n    delay(SAMPLE_GAP_MS);\n  }\n  return sum / SAMPLES;\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);      // start in the safe OFF state\n  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  dht.begin();\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  float temperature = dht.readTemperature();   // degrees C\n  float humidity    = dht.readHumidity();      // percent\n  int lightRaw      = analogRead(LDR_PIN);     // one single reading: jumpy\n  int light         = readLightAveraged();     // 15 readings averaged: steady\n\n  bool relayOn = light > threshold;\n  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, relayOn ? RELAY_ON : RELAY_OFF);\n\n  if (isnan(temperature) || isnan(humidity)) {\n    Serial.println(\"DHT11 read failed - check the wiring\");\n  } else {\n    Serial.printf(\"Temp %.1f C | Humidity %.0f %% | Light raw %d -> averaged %d | Relay %s\\n\",\n                  temperature, humidity, lightRaw, light, relayOn ? \"ON\" : \"OFF\");\n  }\n  delay(2000);                                 // the DHT11 needs at least 2 s between reads\n}\n"}},{"t":"Watch the averaging work","d":["Look at the Serial Monitor. The “raw” light value is a single reading and jumps around; the “averaged” value is the mean of 15 readings taken 8ms apart, and it is calm. Cover the LDR with your hand: the number goes up. Shine a light on it: the number goes down."]},{"t":"Set your threshold","d":["Note the averaged light value in a bright room and in a dark room, and pick a number between them for `threshold` at the top of the code. Upload again. When the averaged reading is above the threshold, the relay switches on by itself; when it drops back, the relay switches off. That is automatic mode."]},{"t":"If it does not work","d":[],"table":{"head":["What you see","What it usually is","What to do"],"rows":[["“DHT11 read failed”","Wiring, or reading it too fast","Check VCC, DATA (GPIO4) and GND. The sensor needs at least 2 seconds between reads."],["The light value never changes","The LDR divider is not connected to GPIO34","Re-check the three connections of the divider."],["The relay is the wrong way round","Polarity","Swap `RELAY_ON` and `RELAY_OFF`, as in Task 2."]]}}]}],"story":["Task 4 built the system I would actually want to run 24/7. I turned the ESP32 into an environmental station reading temperature and humidity from a DHT11 and ambient light from an LDR, with the same relay controlling the bulb. Reading the real world turned out to be messy — the raw light value jumped from 500 to 2000 to 300 with no reason, so I replaced a single reading with 15 samples spaced 8ms apart and averaged them, which gave me a stable value and far better accuracy than the hardware filter capacitor I first considered.","The DHT11 also taught me patience: it needs at least two seconds between reads and quietly drifts unless you average, and it stabilized nicely once I did. The relay got two modes — a manual override from the dashboard, and an automatic mode that watches the light level and switches the bulb itself above a threshold I could tune from 0-4095, always defaulting to OFF if anything disconnected.","Every two seconds the firmware pushed a reading of temperature, humidity, light, and relay state to the cloud — 43,200 data points a day — and I spent a good part of the build double-checking things like the ESP32’s analog input being 12-bit rather than the Arduino’s 10-bit. Getting the data to the cloud reliably was the goal, and testing confirmed it: steady readings, about a 1-2 second sync, and a ~10ms relay response."],"media":[{"type":"image","title":"Task 4 — Forge build","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-4/1.png","caption":"Forge: the ESP32 environmental station — sensors in, relay out, cloud in between.","poster":null,"w":2560,"h":1600,"v":[640,1280]},{"type":"image","title":"Task 4.1 — Sensor hardware","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-4/2.png","caption":"DHT11 temperature & humidity plus the LDR light sensor wired to the ESP32.","poster":null,"w":2560,"h":1600,"v":[640,1280]}],"notes":["Task 4 taught me that reading the real world is messy. My light sensor jumped all over the place — 500, then 2000, then 300 — so I stopped blaming the hardware and started averaging 15 samples spaced 8ms apart, which gave me a smooth, trustworthy reading and proved the value of solving problems in software. The DHT11 forced me to respect its timing and drift before its readings stabilized to well under a degree. Giving the relay two modes felt like real product design: a manual button for me, and an automatic mode that watches the light threshold and switches itself, always falling back to OFF if the connection drops. And I made a habit of double-checking every spec — the ESP32’s analog input is 12-bit, not the Arduino’s 10-bit — because assuming wrong is how silent bugs are born. By the end I had a loop pushing temperature, humidity, light, and relay state every couple of seconds, over 40,000 data points a day, and it ran and ran without complaint."],"id":"4.1","title":"Forge: Sensors & Relay — the Source of the Data","short":"Sensors","sub":"DHT11 + LDR sensors, 15-sample averaging, automatic relay logic"},{"terms":[["Firebase","Google’s cloud platform for apps. Here it stores the data, signs users in and keeps everything in sync. Every reading, bulb state and mode change flows through it in real time."],["Realtime Database","Firebase’s database for live data. It stores everything as one big tree of JSON, and connected devices are told about changes within a moment."],["NoSQL","A database without tables, rows and joins. You store nested data and read it by its path."],["Flat schema","Keeping the data shallow: a few top-level paths instead of deep nesting. A deeply nested first design made queries crawl; flattening it made them instant."],["Timestamp key","Each reading is stored under the time it was taken, so readings are naturally in order."],["Authentication","Proving who a user is. Forge signs users in with email and password or with Google Sign-In."],["Security rules","The rules that decide who can read or write each path. Here every user can only ever read their own data, and readings can only be written by the firmware."],["Cloud functions","Small programs that run in the cloud when something happens. Forge uses them to archive readings older than 30 days, raise an alert above 40°C, keep an audit trail of every bulb command, and serve history to the dashboard."],["Audit trail","A permanent log of who did what and when. Here it records every bulb command."],["Firestore","Firebase’s other database, better for complex per-user documents. It was not the right fit for a stream of time-series readings."],["Latency","The delay between something happening and its result appearing. Reads take 50–200ms and streams update in under a second."]],"guide":[{"h":"Stage A — Create the Firebase project","steps":[{"t":"Make the project","d":["Go to [console.firebase.google.com](https://console.firebase.google.com) and sign in with a Google account. Choose Create a project, name it (for example `forge`), turn Google Analytics off (you do not need it), and press Create project."]},{"t":"Create the Realtime Database","d":["In the left menu open Build → Realtime Database and press Create Database. Choose the location closest to you and start in locked mode. When it opens, copy the database address at the top of the Data tab. It looks like `https://forge-default-rtdb.firebaseio.com`, or ends in `firebasedatabase.app`."]},{"t":"Turn on sign-in","d":["Open Build → Authentication → Get started, then the Sign-in method tab. Enable Email/Password. Then add the Google provider, choose your support email, and Save."]},{"t":"Create two users","d":["Open the Users tab and press Add user twice. The first is you — your email and a password — and it is the account you will use to sign in to the dashboard. The second is the ESP32’s own account, for example `forge-device@example.com` with a password. Firebase does not need this email to be real.","Copy the User UID of your own account from the table. You will need it in a moment."],"tip":"Two accounts is what lets the security rules say “only the device can write readings”."},{"t":"Copy the web settings","d":["Open Project settings (the gear icon) → General. Under Your apps press the web icon `</>`, give the app a name, and press Register app. Copy the `firebaseConfig` block it shows — this is the real version of the placeholder you left in the dashboard’s `firebase-config.js` back in Task 4, so go back and drop these values in now. The `apiKey` value in it is also the key the ESP32 uses."]}]},{"h":"Stage B — Design the data and lock it down","steps":[{"t":"The shape of the data","d":["Keep the data flat: a few paths under each user’s own id, and nothing nested deeper than it has to be."],"code":{"name":"The database tree","lang":"Structure","text":"users\n └ <your user id>\n    ├ bulb: false\n    ├ settings\n    │    ├ mode: \"manual\"          (or \"auto\")\n    │    └ threshold: 2000\n    └ readings\n         └ <unix time>: { temperature, humidity, light, relay }"}},{"t":"Publish the security rules","d":["Open Realtime Database → Rules, delete what is there, paste the rules below, replace both `YOUR_DEVICE_EMAIL` with the ESP32 account’s email, and press Publish."],"code":{"name":"database.rules.json","lang":"JSON","text":"{\n  \"rules\": {\n    \"users\": {\n      \"$uid\": {\n        \".read\": \"auth != null && (auth.uid === $uid || auth.token.email === 'YOUR_DEVICE_EMAIL')\",\n        \"bulb\": {\n          \".write\": \"auth != null && auth.uid === $uid\",\n          \".validate\": \"newData.isBoolean()\"\n        },\n        \"settings\": {\n          \".write\": \"auth != null && auth.uid === $uid\",\n          \"mode\": { \".validate\": \"newData.isString() && (newData.val() === 'manual' || newData.val() === 'auto')\" },\n          \"threshold\": { \".validate\": \"newData.isNumber() && newData.val() >= 0 && newData.val() <= 4095\" }\n        },\n        \"readings\": {\n          \".write\": \"auth != null && auth.token.email === 'YOUR_DEVICE_EMAIL'\"\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n"},"after":["In plain words: a user can read only their own branch (and the ESP32 account can read it too, to fetch the controls). Only that user can change the bulb and the settings, and the values are checked so nothing odd can be stored. Only the ESP32 account can write readings, so sensor data can never be typed in by hand."]},{"t":"Test the rules","d":["In the Rules tab open the Rules Playground. Simulate a read at `/users/someone-elses-id` while authenticated with your own account: it should be denied. Then a read at `/users/<your user id>`: it should be allowed."]}]},{"h":"Stage C — Flash the final firmware","steps":[{"t":"Install the Firebase library","d":["In Manage Libraries, search for `Firebase Arduino Client Library for ESP8266 and ESP32` by Mobizt and install it."]},{"t":"Paste the code and fill in seven values","d":["This is the whole ESP32 program: the sensors and relay from Task 4.1 plus the cloud. Fill in the values at the top: your WiFi name and password, the `apiKey`, the database address, the device account’s email and password, and your own User UID. Upload it and open the Serial Monitor at 115200 baud."],"expect":"Sent | 27.4 C | 58 % | light 1934 | relay OFF | mode manual","code":{"name":"task4_forge.ino","lang":"C++ · Arduino","text":"/* Task 4.2 - Forge firmware (sensors + relay + Firebase)\n   Every 2 seconds the ESP32:\n     1. reads the controls from Firebase  (bulb, mode, threshold)\n     2. reads the DHT11 and the averaged LDR\n     3. switches the relay (manual = follow the dashboard toggle, auto = light vs threshold)\n     4. writes one reading (temperature, humidity, light, relay) keyed by its timestamp\n   If WiFi or Firebase is lost, the relay falls back to OFF. */\n\n#include <WiFi.h>\n#include <time.h>\n#include <DHT.h>\n#include <Firebase_ESP_Client.h>\n#include <addons/TokenHelper.h>\n\n// ---------- fill these in ----------\n#define WIFI_SSID       \"YOUR_WIFI_NAME\"\n#define WIFI_PASSWORD   \"YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD\"\n#define API_KEY         \"YOUR_FIREBASE_WEB_API_KEY\"\n#define DATABASE_URL    \"https://YOUR-PROJECT-default-rtdb.firebaseio.com\"\n#define DEVICE_EMAIL    \"YOUR_DEVICE_EMAIL\"        // the second user you created for the ESP32\n#define DEVICE_PASSWORD \"YOUR_DEVICE_PASSWORD\"\n#define OWNER_UID       \"YOUR_OWN_USER_UID\"        // the UID of YOUR account (the dashboard login)\n// -----------------------------------\n\nconst int DHT_PIN   = 4;\nconst int LDR_PIN   = 34;\nconst int RELAY_PIN = 26;\nconst int RELAY_ON  = LOW;               // swap if your relay module is the opposite\nconst int RELAY_OFF = HIGH;\n\nconst int SAMPLES               = 15;    // light readings averaged into one value\nconst int SAMPLE_GAP_MS         = 8;\nconst unsigned long CYCLE_MS    = 2000;  // one reading every 2 s = 43,200 a day\n\nDHT dht(DHT_PIN, DHT11);\nFirebaseData fbdo;\nFirebaseAuth auth;\nFirebaseConfig config;\n\nString base = String(\"/users/\") + OWNER_UID;   // everything lives under your own user id\nbool   bulbWanted = false;                     // the dashboard toggle\nString mode       = \"manual\";                  // \"manual\" or \"auto\"\nint    threshold  = 2000;                      // 0-4095\nbool   relayOn    = false;\nunsigned long lastCycle = 0;\n\nvoid setRelay(bool on) {\n  relayOn = on;\n  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, on ? RELAY_ON : RELAY_OFF);\n}\n\nint readLight() {\n  long sum = 0;\n  for (int i = 0; i < SAMPLES; i++) {\n    sum += analogRead(LDR_PIN);\n    delay(SAMPLE_GAP_MS);\n  }\n  return sum / SAMPLES;\n}\n\nvoid connectWiFi() {\n  Serial.print(\"Connecting to WiFi\");\n  WiFi.mode(WIFI_STA);\n  WiFi.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASSWORD);\n  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {\n    delay(500);\n    Serial.print(\".\");\n  }\n  Serial.println();\n  Serial.println(\"WiFi connected\");\n}\n\nvoid setup() {\n  Serial.begin(115200);\n  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, RELAY_OFF);      // safe OFF from the very first moment\n  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);\n  dht.begin();\n\n  connectWiFi();\n\n  // The reading's key is the current time, so the clock must be right first.\n  configTime(0, 0, \"pool.ntp.org\", \"time.nist.gov\");\n  Serial.print(\"Syncing the clock\");\n  while (time(nullptr) < 1700000000) {\n    delay(300);\n    Serial.print(\".\");\n  }\n  Serial.println();\n\n  config.api_key      = API_KEY;\n  config.database_url = DATABASE_URL;\n  auth.user.email     = DEVICE_EMAIL;\n  auth.user.password  = DEVICE_PASSWORD;\n  config.token_status_callback = tokenStatusCallback;   // prints sign-in progress\n  Firebase.reconnectWiFi(true);\n  Firebase.begin(&config, &auth);\n}\n\nvoid loop() {\n  if (millis() - lastCycle < CYCLE_MS) return;\n  lastCycle = millis();\n\n  if (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {\n    setRelay(false);                       // no network: safe OFF\n    connectWiFi();\n    return;\n  }\n  if (!Firebase.ready()) {\n    setRelay(false);                       // not signed in yet: safe OFF\n    return;\n  }\n\n  // 1. controls from the dashboard (if one read fails we simply keep the last value)\n  if (Firebase.RTDB.getBool(&fbdo, (base + \"/bulb\").c_str()))            bulbWanted = fbdo.boolData();\n  if (Firebase.RTDB.getString(&fbdo, (base + \"/settings/mode\").c_str())) mode       = fbdo.stringData();\n  if (Firebase.RTDB.getInt(&fbdo, (base + \"/settings/threshold\").c_str())) threshold = fbdo.intData();\n\n  // 2. sensors\n  float temperature = dht.readTemperature();\n  float humidity    = dht.readHumidity();\n  int   light       = readLight();\n\n  // 3. relay\n  if (mode == \"auto\") setRelay(light > threshold);\n  else                setRelay(bulbWanted);\n\n  // 4. one reading up to the cloud\n  if (isnan(temperature) || isnan(humidity)) {\n    Serial.println(\"DHT11 read failed - skipping this reading\");\n    return;\n  }\n  FirebaseJson reading;\n  reading.set(\"temperature\", temperature);\n  reading.set(\"humidity\", humidity);\n  reading.set(\"light\", light);\n  reading.set(\"relay\", relayOn);\n\n  String path = base + \"/readings/\" + String((unsigned long)time(nullptr));\n  if (Firebase.RTDB.setJSON(&fbdo, path.c_str(), &reading)) {\n    Serial.printf(\"Sent | %.1f C | %.0f %% | light %d | relay %s | mode %s\\n\",\n                  temperature, humidity, light, relayOn ? \"ON\" : \"OFF\", mode.c_str());\n  } else {\n    Serial.print(\"Send failed: \");\n    Serial.println(fbdo.errorReason());\n  }\n}\n"}},{"t":"Watch the data arrive","d":["In the Firebase console open Realtime Database → Data and expand `users` → your id → `readings`. A new entry appears every two seconds, named after the time it was taken."],"tip":"Nothing arrives? Read the Serial Monitor: an authentication error means the device email or password is wrong, and a permission error means the rules or the User UID need checking."},{"t":"Try the controls by hand","d":["In the Data tab, add `bulb` with the value `true` under your user id. With the relay wired, the ESP32 switches it on within a couple of seconds. Set it back to `false`. Then add `settings` → `mode` = `auto` and the relay follows the light instead. The dashboard you build in Task 4.3 does exactly this for you, and Task 5 is where you watch it happen live."]}]},{"h":"Stage D — Optional: cloud functions","steps":[{"t":"What they do and what they need","d":["These three small functions do the housekeeping: raise an alert above 40°C, keep an audit trail of every bulb command, and archive readings older than 30 days. The dashboard reads its history straight from the `readings` path, so nothing else is needed for it."],"warn":["Deploying cloud functions needs Firebase’s pay-as-you-go plan (Blaze), which you must add billing to, even though it has a free monthly allowance. Everything else in this guide works on the free plan, so skip this stage if you do not want that."]},{"t":"Deploy them","d":["Install [Node.js](https://nodejs.org), then in a terminal run these commands. Choose your project, JavaScript, and install the dependencies when asked."],"code":{"name":"Terminal","lang":"Shell","text":"npm install -g firebase-tools\nfirebase login\nfirebase init functions"}},{"t":"Paste the functions and deploy","d":["Replace the contents of `functions/index.js` with this code. If your database is not in the US, fill in the `WHERE` line as its comment says. Then run `firebase deploy --only functions`."],"code":{"name":"index.js","lang":"JavaScript","text":"// functions/index.js - optional housekeeping for Forge (Firebase Cloud Functions, 2nd gen)\nconst { onValueCreated, onValueWritten } = require(\"firebase-functions/v2/database\");\nconst { onSchedule } = require(\"firebase-functions/v2/scheduler\");\nconst admin = require(\"firebase-admin\");\n\nadmin.initializeApp();\nconst db = admin.database();\n\n// If your Realtime Database is NOT in the US, name its region and instance here, e.g.\n// const WHERE = { region: \"asia-southeast1\", instance: \"YOUR-PROJECT-default-rtdb\" };\nconst WHERE = {};\n\n// 1. Alert: write a note whenever a reading is above 40 degrees C.\nexports.temperatureAlert = onValueCreated({ ref: \"/users/{uid}/readings/{ts}\", ...WHERE }, async (event) => {\n  const reading = event.data.val();\n  if (reading && reading.temperature > 40) {\n    await db.ref(`/users/${event.params.uid}/alerts/${event.params.ts}`).set({\n      temperature: reading.temperature,\n      message: \"Temperature above 40 C\"\n    });\n  }\n});\n\n// 2. Audit trail: remember every change of the bulb command.\nexports.bulbAudit = onValueWritten({ ref: \"/users/{uid}/bulb\", ...WHERE }, async (event) => {\n  await db.ref(`/users/${event.params.uid}/audit`).push({\n    bulb: event.data.after.val(),\n    at: admin.database.ServerValue.TIMESTAMP\n  });\n});\n\n// 3. Archive: once a day, move readings older than 30 days to /archive.\nexports.archiveOldReadings = onSchedule(\"every 24 hours\", async () => {\n  const cutoff = String(Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) - 30 * 24 * 60 * 60);\n  const users = await db.ref(\"/users\").once(\"value\");\n  const jobs = [];\n  users.forEach((user) => {\n    jobs.push((async () => {\n      const old = await db.ref(`/users/${user.key}/readings`).orderByKey().endAt(cutoff).once(\"value\");\n      const updates = {};\n      old.forEach((row) => {\n        updates[`/users/${user.key}/archive/${row.key}`] = row.val();\n        updates[`/users/${user.key}/readings/${row.key}`] = null;\n      });\n      if (Object.keys(updates).length) await db.ref().update(updates);\n    })());\n  });\n  await Promise.all(jobs);\n});\n"}}]}],"story":["If the ESP32 was the brain, Firebase became the nervous system — every sensor reading, bulb state, and mode change flowed through it in real time. My first schema design was a mistake I made and fixed in about 30 minutes: I nested paths deeply, the queries crawled, and I learned the hard way that Firebase wants flat data. I flattened everything to a few top-level paths — sensor readings keyed by timestamp, the bulb toggle, and settings for mode and light threshold — and queries became instant.","Security was the part that made me feel like a real engineer: I configured email/password and Google Sign-In, then wrote rules so every user could only ever read their own data, and so sensor data could never be written by hand — only by the firmware. Real-time listeners meant a change on the hardware side appeared on the dashboard within about a second, the same architecture that powers Philips Hue and Ecobee.","I also wrote cloud functions that archived readings older than 30 days, raised an alert above 40°C, kept an audit trail of every bulb command, and served history to the dashboard. The measured behavior — 50-200ms reads, sub-second streams, extremely stable connections — is why I chose Realtime Database over Firestore: this workload is exactly what RTDB is built for."],"media":[{"type":"image","title":"Task 4.2 — Cloud backend","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-4/3.png","caption":"Firebase Realtime Database — the flattened schema, auth, and security rules behind Forge.","poster":null,"w":2556,"h":1596,"v":[640,1280]}],"notes":["Firebase was the nervous system that tied the hardware to the screen, and it handed me my first real architecture failure. I started with a deeply nested database structure, watched the queries crawl, and spent about half an hour tearing it down into a flat layout — readings by timestamp, a bulb state, and settings — and instantly everything got fast. What made me feel like this was production work was the security layer: I set up email and Google sign-in, then wrote rules so each user only ever saw their own data and sensor records could never be hand-written, only pushed by the firmware. The real-time listeners were the satisfying part — flip the toggle on the dashboard and the hardware reacts in about a second, in both directions. I added cloud functions for housekeeping too: archiving old readings, alerting on high temperature, logging every bulb command, and serving history to the UI. Choosing RTDB over Firestore came down to the workload: time-series streams with live listeners are exactly what it is designed for.","Choosing the cloud backend forced me to think, not copy. Firebase Realtime Database won because my workload is live time-series streams from one sensor station with listeners on the dashboard — exactly what RTDB was made for, including low-latency fan-out and massive concurrent connections. Firestore would have been better for complex per-user documents, but that is not this system. And the build order I chose mattered as much as the stack: firmware and hardware first, then the cloud, then the UI, so that at every single moment only one layer could be the unknown. That rule saved me days of frustrating guesswork."],"id":"4.2","title":"Forge: Firebase Real-Time Cloud Backend","short":"Firebase","sub":"Firebase Realtime Database, authentication, security rules, cloud functions"},{"terms":[["Dashboard","The web page you use to see the sensor data and control the bulb. Here it is a small three-page web app, built on its own before any hardware is connected."],["Three-page web app","A landing page announcing Forge, a login page, and the dashboard itself."],["HTML, CSS and JavaScript","The three languages of every web page: HTML is the structure, CSS is the look, JavaScript is the behaviour. Forge uses only these, with no framework."],["State management","Keeping track of what the page currently knows: for example whether the bulb toggle is a manual command or the automatic mode’s decision."],["Mode selector","The control that switches between manual and automatic."],["Light-sensitivity slider","The slider that sets the threshold (0–4095) used in automatic mode."],["History table","The list of the latest recorded readings."],["CSV export","One click downloads the recorded temperature, humidity and light as a spreadsheet file."],["Input validation","Checking what a user types before it is used, so bad values never reach the database."],["Loading and error states","What the page shows while data is still arriving, and what it says when something goes wrong, instead of a blank screen."],["Responsive CSS","Styles that adapt to the screen size. The dashboard was tested on phone, tablet and desktop."],["Transition animation","A short smooth change between two states. Without it the toggle felt dead; with it the same response felt instant."],["Perceived performance","How fast something feels rather than how fast it is. Users do not care about your stack, they care that clicking ON turns the light on."]],"guide":[{"h":"Stage A — Set up the project","steps":[{"t":"Gather your tools","d":[],"list":["A text editor. [Visual Studio Code](https://code.visualstudio.com) is a good free one.","Google Chrome.","Python (already installed if you followed the earlier tasks), only to run a small local web server."]},{"t":"Make the folder and files","d":["Create a folder named `forge-dashboard`. Inside it create these six files, and paste in the code from the steps below."],"code":{"name":"The folder","lang":"Structure","text":"forge-dashboard/\n ├ index.html          the landing page\n ├ login.html          sign in\n ├ dashboard.html      the live dashboard\n ├ style.css           the look of all three\n └ firebase-config.js  your Firebase settings"}},{"t":"Add your Firebase settings","d":["Paste your own values from Part 4.2 (the `firebaseConfig` block) into `firebase-config.js`. You created that project earlier in this same task, so the real values already exist — no placeholders needed here."],"code":{"name":"firebase-config.js","lang":"JavaScript","text":"// Paste YOUR values from Firebase Console > Project settings > General > Your apps.\nimport { initializeApp } from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.2/firebase-app.js\";\nimport { getAuth } from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.2/firebase-auth.js\";\nimport { getDatabase } from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.2/firebase-database.js\";\n\nconst firebaseConfig = {\n  apiKey: \"YOUR_API_KEY\",\n  authDomain: \"YOUR-PROJECT.firebaseapp.com\",\n  databaseURL: \"https://YOUR-PROJECT-default-rtdb.firebaseio.com\",\n  projectId: \"YOUR-PROJECT\",\n  appId: \"YOUR_APP_ID\"\n};\n\nexport const app  = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);\nexport const auth = getAuth(app);\nexport const db   = getDatabase(app);\n"}}]},{"h":"Stage B — The look","steps":[{"t":"Add the stylesheet","d":["This one file styles all three pages. It is responsive: below 760 pixels wide the cards stack into one column, and it respects the “reduce motion” setting."],"code":{"name":"style.css","lang":"CSS","text":":root{\n  --bg:#0b0b0c; --card:#151517; --line:#2a2a2e; --text:#f2f0ef; --muted:#9a9a9f;\n  --accent:#dc4a15; --glow:#f1923e; --ok:#3ecf8e;\n}\n*{box-sizing:border-box}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--text);font:16px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif}\na{color:inherit}\nh1,h2,h3{margin:0;line-height:1.15}\n\n/* layout */\n.center{min-height:100vh;display:grid;place-items:center;padding:24px}\n.wrap{max-width:1000px;margin:0 auto;padding:24px 16px 64px}\n.top{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin-bottom:24px}\n.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:14px}\n.card,.panel{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;padding:20px}\n.panel{width:100%;max-width:380px;display:grid;gap:14px}\n.card small{color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;font-size:12px}\n.value{font-size:44px;font-weight:600;margin-top:6px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}\n.value span{font-size:18px;color:var(--muted);margin-left:4px}\n.row{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap}\nsection{margin-top:14px}\n\n/* forms + buttons */\nlabel{display:grid;gap:6px;color:var(--muted);font-size:14px}\ninput[type=email],input[type=password]{background:#0f0f10;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:12px;color:var(--text);font:inherit}\ninput:focus-visible,button:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--glow);outline-offset:2px}\n.btn{background:var(--text);color:#000;border:0;border-radius:999px;padding:12px 22px;font:inherit;font-weight:600;cursor:pointer;text-decoration:none;display:inline-block;text-align:center}\n.btn.ghost{background:transparent;color:var(--text);border:1px solid var(--line)}\n.btn:disabled{opacity:.5;cursor:wait}\n.msg{min-height:1.4em;margin:0;color:#ff7a59;font-size:14px}\n\n/* bulb + toggle */\n.bulb{width:64px;height:64px;fill:none;transition:filter .4s,opacity .4s,fill .4s;opacity:.35}\n.bulb.on{opacity:1;fill:rgba(241,146,62,.35);filter:drop-shadow(0 0 14px var(--glow))}\n.switch{position:relative;display:inline-block;width:64px;height:34px;flex:none}\n.switch input{position:absolute;inset:0;opacity:0;margin:0;cursor:pointer;z-index:1}\n.switch i{position:absolute;inset:0;background:#333;border-radius:999px;transition:background .3s}\n.switch i::after{content:\"\";position:absolute;top:4px;left:4px;width:26px;height:26px;border-radius:50%;background:#fff;transition:transform .3s}\n.switch input:checked + i{background:var(--accent)}\n.switch input:checked + i::after{transform:translateX(30px)}\n.switch input:disabled{cursor:not-allowed}\n.switch input:disabled + i{opacity:.45}\n.switch input:focus-visible + i{outline:2px solid var(--glow);outline-offset:3px}\n\n/* mode selector + slider */\n.seg{display:inline-flex;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:999px;padding:3px}\n.seg button{background:none;border:0;color:var(--muted);padding:8px 18px;border-radius:999px;font:inherit;cursor:pointer}\n.seg button[aria-pressed=true]{background:var(--text);color:#000}\ninput[type=range]{width:100%;accent-color:var(--accent)}\n\n/* status + history */\n.status{color:var(--muted);font-size:14px}\n.status b{display:inline-block;width:8px;height:8px;border-radius:50%;background:#666;margin-right:8px}\n.status.live b{background:var(--ok)}\n.tablewrap{overflow-x:auto}\ntable{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;font-size:14px}\nth,td{text-align:left;padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);white-space:nowrap}\nth{color:var(--muted);font-weight:500}\n\n/* phones and tablets */\n@media (max-width:760px){.grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.value{font-size:38px}}\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{transition:none!important}}\n"}}]},{"h":"Stage C — The three pages","steps":[{"t":"The landing page","d":["A simple page announcing Forge, with a button to sign in."],"code":{"name":"index.html","lang":"HTML","text":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>Forge</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n</head>\n<body class=\"center\">\n  <main style=\"text-align:center;max-width:560px\">\n    <h1 style=\"font-size:64px\">Forge</h1>\n    <p style=\"color:var(--muted)\">A live look at temperature, humidity and light, and one switch for the bulb - from any screen.</p>\n    <p><a class=\"btn\" href=\"login.html\">Sign in</a></p>\n  </main>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}},{"t":"The login page","d":["It signs in with email and password or with Google, validates what you type, shows a friendly message when something is wrong, and sends you straight to the dashboard if you are already signed in."],"code":{"name":"login.html","lang":"HTML","text":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>Forge - Sign in</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n</head>\n<body class=\"center\">\n  <form id=\"form\" class=\"panel\" novalidate>\n    <h1>Sign in to Forge</h1>\n    <label>Email\n      <input id=\"email\" type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\" required>\n    </label>\n    <label>Password\n      <input id=\"password\" type=\"password\" autocomplete=\"current-password\" required minlength=\"6\">\n    </label>\n    <p id=\"msg\" class=\"msg\" role=\"alert\"></p>\n    <button class=\"btn\" id=\"signin\" type=\"submit\">Sign in</button>\n    <button class=\"btn ghost\" id=\"google\" type=\"button\">Continue with Google</button>\n  </form>\n\n  <script type=\"module\">\n    import { auth } from \"./firebase-config.js\";\n    import { signInWithEmailAndPassword, signInWithPopup, GoogleAuthProvider, onAuthStateChanged }\n      from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.2/firebase-auth.js\";\n\n    const $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);\n    const msg = $(\"msg\"), signin = $(\"signin\"), google = $(\"google\");\n\n    // already signed in? go straight to the dashboard\n    onAuthStateChanged(auth, (user) => { if (user) location.replace(\"dashboard.html\"); });\n\n    const busy = (on) => { signin.disabled = google.disabled = on; signin.textContent = on ? \"Signing in...\" : \"Sign in\"; };\n    const friendly = (code) => ({\n      \"auth/invalid-email\": \"That email address is not valid.\",\n      \"auth/invalid-credential\": \"Wrong email or password.\",\n      \"auth/user-not-found\": \"Wrong email or password.\",\n      \"auth/wrong-password\": \"Wrong email or password.\",\n      \"auth/too-many-requests\": \"Too many attempts. Wait a minute and try again.\",\n      \"auth/network-request-failed\": \"No connection. Check your internet and try again.\",\n      \"auth/popup-closed-by-user\": \"The Google window was closed before finishing.\"\n    })[code] || \"Could not sign in. Please try again.\";\n    const fail = (e) => { msg.textContent = friendly(e.code); busy(false); };\n\n    $(\"form\").addEventListener(\"submit\", (e) => {\n      e.preventDefault();\n      msg.textContent = \"\";\n      const email = $(\"email\").value.trim(), password = $(\"password\").value;\n      if (!/^\\S+@\\S+\\.\\S+$/.test(email)) { msg.textContent = \"Enter a valid email address.\"; return; }\n      if (password.length < 6) { msg.textContent = \"The password is at least 6 characters.\"; return; }\n      busy(true);\n      signInWithEmailAndPassword(auth, email, password).catch(fail);   // success is handled by onAuthStateChanged\n    });\n\n    google.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n      msg.textContent = \"\";\n      busy(true);\n      signInWithPopup(auth, new GoogleAuthProvider()).catch(fail);\n    });\n  </script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}},{"t":"The dashboard","d":["Everything happens here: live cards for temperature, humidity and light, an animated bulb toggle, the manual/automatic mode selector, the light-sensitivity slider, the history table and the one-click CSV export. Real-time listeners keep it up to date without a refresh, and it redirects to the login page if nobody is signed in."],"code":{"name":"dashboard.html","lang":"HTML","text":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>Forge - Dashboard</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"wrap\">\n  <header class=\"top\">\n    <h1>Forge</h1>\n    <div class=\"row\">\n      <span id=\"who\" class=\"status\"></span>\n      <button id=\"out\" class=\"btn ghost\" type=\"button\">Sign out</button>\n    </div>\n  </header>\n\n  <p id=\"status\" class=\"status\"><b></b><span>Connecting...</span></p>\n  <p id=\"err\" class=\"msg\" role=\"alert\"></p>\n\n  <div class=\"grid\">\n    <div class=\"card\"><small>Temperature</small><div class=\"value\"><em id=\"temp\">--</em><span>&deg;C</span></div></div>\n    <div class=\"card\"><small>Humidity</small><div class=\"value\"><em id=\"hum\">--</em><span>%</span></div></div>\n    <div class=\"card\"><small>Light (0-4095)</small><div class=\"value\"><em id=\"light\">--</em></div></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <section class=\"card\">\n    <div class=\"row\">\n      <div class=\"row\" style=\"gap:16px\">\n        <svg id=\"bulb\" class=\"bulb\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" stroke=\"#f1923e\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n          <path d=\"M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-3.6 10.8c.6.5 1 1.2 1 2V16h5.2v-.2c0-.8.4-1.5 1-2A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z\"/>\n        </svg>\n        <div><h2>Bulb</h2><div id=\"bulbNote\" class=\"status\">--</div></div>\n      </div>\n      <span class=\"switch\"><input id=\"toggle\" type=\"checkbox\" role=\"switch\" aria-label=\"Bulb on or off\"><i></i></span>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <section class=\"card\">\n    <div class=\"row\">\n      <div><h2>Mode</h2><div class=\"status\">Manual follows the toggle. Automatic follows the light.</div></div>\n      <div class=\"seg\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Mode\">\n        <button type=\"button\" data-mode=\"manual\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Manual</button>\n        <button type=\"button\" data-mode=\"auto\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Automatic</button>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"margin-top:18px\">\n      <div class=\"row\"><label for=\"range\" style=\"display:inline\">Light sensitivity (threshold)</label><strong id=\"rangeOut\">2000</strong></div>\n      <input id=\"range\" type=\"range\" min=\"0\" max=\"4095\" step=\"1\" value=\"2000\">\n      <div class=\"status\">In automatic mode the bulb switches on when the light reading is above this number.</div>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n\n  <section class=\"card\">\n    <div class=\"row\" style=\"margin-bottom:12px\">\n      <h2>History</h2>\n      <button id=\"csv\" class=\"btn ghost\" type=\"button\">Export CSV</button>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"tablewrap\">\n      <table>\n        <thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Temp &deg;C</th><th>Humidity %</th><th>Light</th><th>Bulb</th></tr></thead>\n        <tbody id=\"rows\"><tr><td colspan=\"5\" class=\"status\">Waiting for the first reading...</td></tr></tbody>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n</div>\n\n<script type=\"module\">\n  import { auth, db } from \"./firebase-config.js\";\n  import { onAuthStateChanged, signOut } from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.2/firebase-auth.js\";\n  import { ref, onValue, set, update, get, query, limitToLast }\n    from \"https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/10.12.2/firebase-database.js\";\n\n  const $ = (id) => document.getElementById(id);\n  let base = \"\", started = false;\n  let bulbWanted = false, mode = \"manual\", threshold = 2000, latest = null, history = [];\n\n  const say = (text) => { $(\"err\").textContent = text || \"\"; };\n  const readError = () => say(\"Cannot read your data. Check that you are signed in and that the security rules are published.\");\n  const writeError = () => say(\"That change could not be sent. Check your connection and try again.\");\n  const num = (v, digits) => (typeof v === \"number\" && isFinite(v)) ? v.toFixed(digits) : \"--\";\n\n  // ---- sign in guard ----\n  onAuthStateChanged(auth, (user) => {\n    if (!user) { location.replace(\"login.html\"); return; }\n    base = \"users/\" + user.uid;\n    $(\"who\").textContent = user.email || \"\";\n    start();\n  });\n  $(\"out\").addEventListener(\"click\", () => signOut(auth));\n\n  // ---- live listeners: the page updates itself the moment the data changes ----\n  function start() {\n    if (started) return;\n    started = true;\n\n    onValue(ref(db, base + \"/bulb\"), (snap) => { bulbWanted = snap.val() === true; render(); }, readError);\n\n    onValue(ref(db, base + \"/settings\"), (snap) => {\n      const v = snap.val() || {};\n      mode = v.mode === \"auto\" ? \"auto\" : \"manual\";\n      if (typeof v.threshold === \"number\") threshold = v.threshold;\n      render();\n    }, readError);\n\n    onValue(query(ref(db, base + \"/readings\"), limitToLast(20)), (snap) => {\n      const rows = [];\n      snap.forEach((child) => { rows.push({ ts: Number(child.key), ...child.val() }); });\n      rows.sort((a, b) => a.ts - b.ts);\n      history = rows;\n      latest = rows.length ? rows[rows.length - 1] : null;\n      render();\n    }, readError);\n\n    setInterval(renderStatus, 1000);\n  }\n\n  // ---- drawing ----\n  function render() {\n    say(\"\");\n    $(\"temp\").textContent  = latest ? num(latest.temperature, 1) : \"--\";\n    $(\"hum\").textContent   = latest ? num(latest.humidity, 0) : \"--\";\n    $(\"light\").textContent = latest ? num(latest.light, 0) : \"--\";\n\n    // manual: show what you asked for. automatic: show what the relay is actually doing.\n    const on = mode === \"auto\" ? !!(latest && latest.relay) : bulbWanted;\n    $(\"toggle\").checked = on;\n    $(\"toggle\").disabled = mode === \"auto\";\n    $(\"bulb\").classList.toggle(\"on\", on);\n    $(\"bulbNote\").textContent = mode === \"auto\" ? \"Automatic - the light sensor decides\" : (on ? \"On (manual)\" : \"Off (manual)\");\n\n    document.querySelectorAll(\"[data-mode]\").forEach((b) => b.setAttribute(\"aria-pressed\", String(b.dataset.mode === mode)));\n    if (document.activeElement !== $(\"range\")) { $(\"range\").value = threshold; $(\"rangeOut\").textContent = threshold; }\n\n    const body = $(\"rows\");\n    body.textContent = \"\";\n    if (!history.length) {\n      const tr = document.createElement(\"tr\"), td = document.createElement(\"td\");\n      td.colSpan = 5; td.className = \"status\"; td.textContent = \"Waiting for the first reading...\";\n      tr.append(td); body.append(tr);\n    }\n    history.slice().reverse().forEach((r) => {\n      const tr = document.createElement(\"tr\");\n      [new Date(r.ts * 1000).toLocaleTimeString(), num(r.temperature, 1), num(r.humidity, 0), num(r.light, 0), r.relay ? \"ON\" : \"OFF\"]\n        .forEach((text) => { const td = document.createElement(\"td\"); td.textContent = text; tr.append(td); });\n      body.append(tr);\n    });\n    renderStatus();\n  }\n\n  function renderStatus() {\n    const el = $(\"status\");\n    if (!latest) { el.classList.remove(\"live\"); el.lastChild.textContent = \"Waiting for the first reading...\"; return; }\n    const age = Math.max(0, Math.round(Date.now() / 1000 - latest.ts));\n    const live = age < 15;\n    el.classList.toggle(\"live\", live);\n    el.lastChild.textContent = live ? \"Live - updated \" + age + \" s ago\" : \"Device offline - last reading \" + (age < 3600 ? Math.round(age / 60) + \" min\" : Math.round(age / 3600) + \" h\") + \" ago\";\n  }\n\n  // ---- controls ----\n  $(\"toggle\").addEventListener(\"change\", (e) => {\n    if (mode === \"auto\") return;\n    set(ref(db, base + \"/bulb\"), e.target.checked).catch(writeError);\n  });\n\n  document.querySelectorAll(\"[data-mode]\").forEach((b) => b.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n    update(ref(db, base + \"/settings\"), { mode: b.dataset.mode }).catch(writeError);\n  }));\n\n  $(\"range\").addEventListener(\"input\", (e) => { $(\"rangeOut\").textContent = e.target.value; });\n  $(\"range\").addEventListener(\"change\", (e) => {\n    const v = Math.round(Number(e.target.value));\n    if (!(v >= 0 && v <= 4095)) return;          // the ESP32 ADC only reads 0-4095\n    update(ref(db, base + \"/settings\"), { threshold: v }).catch(writeError);\n  });\n\n  // ---- one-click CSV export (up to the latest 5000 readings) ----\n  $(\"csv\").addEventListener(\"click\", async () => {\n    const btn = $(\"csv\");\n    btn.disabled = true;\n    try {\n      const snap = await get(query(ref(db, base + \"/readings\"), limitToLast(5000)));\n      const rows = [[\"time_iso\", \"unix_time\", \"temperature_c\", \"humidity_percent\", \"light_0_4095\", \"bulb\"]];\n      snap.forEach((child) => {\n        const v = child.val();\n        rows.push([new Date(Number(child.key) * 1000).toISOString(), child.key, v.temperature, v.humidity, v.light, v.relay ? \"ON\" : \"OFF\"]);\n      });\n      if (rows.length === 1) { say(\"There are no readings to export yet.\"); return; }\n      const blob = new Blob([rows.map((r) => r.join(\",\")).join(\"\\n\")], { type: \"text/csv\" });\n      const a = document.createElement(\"a\");\n      a.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);\n      a.download = \"forge-readings.csv\";\n      a.click();\n      URL.revokeObjectURL(a.href);\n    } catch (err) {\n      say(\"Could not export the data. Try again.\");\n    } finally {\n      btn.disabled = false;\n    }\n  });\n</script>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}}]},{"h":"Stage D — Run it and check the interface","steps":[{"t":"Start a local web server","d":["The pages load their scripts as modules, and browsers refuse that when a page is opened straight from a file. Open a terminal inside the `forge-dashboard` folder and run:"],"code":{"name":"Terminal","lang":"Shell","text":"python -m http.server 8000"},"after":["Then open `http://localhost:8000` in Chrome."]},{"t":"Check the pages and the layout","d":["Open the landing page and press Sign in. Sign in with your own account, and again with Google. A wrong password should show a friendly message, and the dashboard itself should open."],"list":["The page keeps its layout as you make the window narrower: the cards stack into one column below 760 pixels.","Everything is readable and nothing overflows on a phone-sized window.","The transitions are smooth, and switch off when your system asks for reduced motion.","If the ESP32 from Part 4.1/4.2 is already powered on, the cards should already be moving on their own — Task 5 is where you slow down and study exactly what that live data is doing."]},{"t":"If it does not work","d":[],"table":{"head":["What you see","What it usually is","What to do"],"rows":[["A blank page and a red error in the browser console","The page was opened straight from a file instead of through the local server","Run the local server and open `http://localhost:8000`."],["The styling is missing","`style.css` is not in the same folder as the pages","Keep all six files together in `forge-dashboard`."],["Nothing on the page is clickable","A script failed to load","Open the browser console and read the first red error."]]}}]}],"story":["The dashboard is where all the complexity had to disappear. I built a clean three-page web app in plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript — a landing page announcing Forge, a login page with email/password and Google Sign-In, and the dashboard itself showing live temperature, humidity, and light cards, a smooth animated bulb toggle, a manual/automatic mode selector, a light-sensitivity slider, a history table, and one-click CSV export.","Behind that simple UI sat Firebase real-time listeners, state management that knew whether a toggle was manual or automatic, input validation, loading and error states, and responsive CSS I tested on four different screen sizes. The biggest lesson of the week came from a detail I almost skipped: I first shipped the dashboard without transition animations and it felt broken — a plain instant switch felt dead.","Once I added smooth transitions, a response that took a full second felt instant, while a 10x faster change with no feedback felt sluggish. That taught me more about perceived performance than any optimization guide: users do not care about your stack, they care that clicking ON turns the light on, and great engineering is the part they never see."],"media":[{"type":"image","title":"Task 4.3 — Web dashboard","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-4/4.png","caption":"The three-page dashboard with live sensor cards and the animated bulb toggle.","poster":null,"w":2560,"h":1598,"v":[640,1280]},{"type":"image","title":"Task 4.3 — Dashboard modes","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-4/5.png","caption":"Manual/automatic mode selector and the light-sensitivity slider controlling the relay.","poster":null,"w":2560,"h":1600,"v":[640,1280]},{"type":"image","title":"Task 4.3 — History & export","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-4/6.png","caption":"The history view with one-click CSV export of recorded temperature, humidity, and light.","poster":null,"w":2560,"h":1600,"v":[640,1280]},{"type":"image","title":"Task 4.3 — Responsive review","src":"assets/protosem/week-7/task-4/7.png","caption":"Forge tested across screen sizes — phone, tablet, and desktop.","poster":null,"w":2560,"h":1600,"v":[640,1280]}],"notes":["The dashboard made me think about users for the first time. Underneath the simple three-page interface sat real complexity — Firebase listeners, state management, validation, error handling, responsive layouts — but the whole point was that nobody should ever see it. People just want the cards to update, the toggle to feel smooth, the mode switch to do the right thing, and the export button to give them their data. My favourite lesson came from an animation: the version without transitions felt broken, and the version with them felt instant even though it was technically slower. I measured, tested on phone/tablet/desktop, and kept a performance bar in mind the whole time. By the end the dashboard felt like a real product, not a class demo."],"id":"4.3","title":"Forge: Web Dashboard & User Interface","short":"Dashboard","sub":"responsive 3-page web app, live listeners, mode selector, CSV export"}]},"5":{"n":5,"title":"Forge: Real-Time Data on the Dashboard","sub":"Bringing the Task 4 build online and watching live sensor data, manual and automatic control, end-to-end proof","meta":[["Data","Real time · 1–2 s latency"],["Control","Manual · Automatic"],["Proof","End-to-end checklist"],["Deploy","Firebase Hosting (optional)"]],"parts":[{"terms":[["Real-time data","Values shown the moment they are produced. A reading leaves the ESP32 and appears on the dashboard within about a second, with no refresh and no polling."],["Real-time listener","Code built into the Task 4 dashboard that is called the moment a value changes in Firebase, so a change on the hardware appears on screen within about a second."],["Live status","The line on the dashboard that says Live when readings are arriving and Waiting when they are not."],["End-to-end","Every link working together — sensor, ESP32, Firebase and dashboard — checked in one pass instead of each on its own."],["Fail-safe","What happens when a link breaks: the status drops to Waiting and the relay returns to OFF, rather than freezing on the last value."],["Firebase Hosting","Firebase’s free web hosting, used to put the finished dashboard on a public address."],["Perceived performance","How fast something feels rather than how fast it is. Users do not care about your stack, they care that clicking ON turns the light on."]],"guide":[{"h":"Stage A — Bring the whole system up","steps":[{"t":"Power on the hardware and cloud from Task 4","d":["Power the ESP32 you finished flashing in Part 4.2 and open its Serial Monitor: you should see `Sent |` lines every two seconds. That firmware and the Firebase project behind it already exist — this task is about watching them work, not building anything new."]},{"t":"Start the dashboard","d":["Open a terminal inside the `forge-dashboard` folder from Part 4.3 and run:"],"code":{"name":"Terminal","lang":"Shell","text":"python -m http.server 8000"},"after":["Then open `http://localhost:8000` in Chrome."]},{"t":"Sign in","d":["Press Sign in on the landing page and sign in with the account you created in Part 4.2, or with Google."]}]},{"h":"Stage B — Watch the real-time data arrive","steps":[{"t":"Watch live data arrive without refreshing","d":["The temperature, humidity and light cards should update on their own every couple of seconds, and the status line should say Live. That is the real-time listener built into the dashboard receiving values the instant the ESP32 writes them — no polling anywhere in the chain, from sensor to screen."],"expect":"Live · temperature, humidity and light changing on their own"},{"t":"Confirm it is your data, not sample data","d":["Warm the DHT11 with your fingers, or cover the LDR with your hand. The matching card changes within a second or two."]}]},{"h":"Stage C — Control the bulb from the dashboard","steps":[{"t":"Manual mode","d":["Flip the bulb toggle. The dashboard writes `bulb` into Firebase, the ESP32 hears it, the relay clicks and the bulb follows within a second or two. Flip it back to switch it off."]},{"t":"Automatic mode","d":["Switch the mode to Automatic. The toggle locks and shows what the relay is actually doing. Move the sensitivity slider and cover the LDR: the bulb switches itself when the light crosses your threshold."]},{"t":"History and export","d":["Let it run for a minute. The history table fills with readings, and Export CSV downloads them as a spreadsheet. Open it: every row is a genuine reading this ESP32 sent."]}]},{"h":"Stage D — Prove the whole chain","steps":[{"t":"Run the end-to-end checklist","d":["Tick each line in order. When all of them pass, sensor, ESP32, Firebase and dashboard are working as one system."],"list":["The ESP32 prints `Sent |` lines every two seconds.","New entries appear under `readings` in the Firebase console.","The dashboard cards update without a refresh and the status says Live.","The manual toggle switches the relay within a second or two.","Automatic mode follows the light, and the toggle locks.","The history table fills and the CSV opens.","The layout stacks neatly on a narrow window or on your phone."]},{"t":"Test what happens when something breaks","d":["Unplug the ESP32. Within a few seconds the status changes from Live to Waiting and the relay goes back to OFF. Plug it back in and the cards start moving again by themselves. This is the safe-by-default behaviour designed into the firmware in Part 4.2."]},{"t":"If it does not work","d":[],"table":{"head":["What you see","What it usually is","What to do"],"rows":[["A blank page and a red error in the browser console","The page was opened from a file, or `firebase-config.js` was never filled in","Run the local server, and re-check Part 4.3’s Stage A."],["“Cannot read your data”","The security rules are not published, or you are signed in as a different user","Publish the rules from Part 4.2 and sign in with your own account."],["The cards say `--` and the status says Waiting","The ESP32 is not sending readings","Check the Serial Monitor for `Sent |` lines, and re-check the device account’s email and password."],["Cards update, but the bulb toggle does nothing","Relay wiring or polarity","Re-check the relay wiring and polarity exactly as in Task 2."],["Automatic mode does not react to light","The threshold is outside the room’s real light range","Note the averaged light value in bright and dark, and set the slider between them."],["Google sign-in fails","The site’s address is not in the authorised domains","Add it under Authentication → Settings → Authorized domains."]]}}]},{"h":"Stage E — Optional: put it online","steps":[{"t":"Deploy with Firebase Hosting","d":["In the `forge-dashboard` folder run these commands. When asked for the public directory type `.`, answer No to a single-page app, and answer No if it offers to overwrite `index.html`."],"code":{"name":"Terminal","lang":"Shell","text":"npm install -g firebase-tools\nfirebase login\nfirebase init hosting\nfirebase deploy --only hosting"},"after":["Firebase prints your live address. Add it under Authorized domains, then sign in there and repeat the checklist."]}]}],"story":[],"media":[],"notes":[]}]}}};
